{"meta": {"limit": 500, "next": null, "offset": 0, "previous": null, "total_count": 21}, "objects": [{"abstract": "   This document defines how parameters can be added to AI Preferences.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-02-07T13:41:26Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 160242, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-zehta-aipref-parameters", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 4, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-zehta-aipref-parameters/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/167396/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-06T13:41:26Z", "title": "AIPREF Vocabulary Parameters", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document proposes a vocabulary for expressing content\n   preferences for rightsholders who wish to manage the use of their\n   content in AI training.  This vocabulary allows publishers to express\n   preferences through metadata or content-delivery protocols.  The\n   vocabulary can be applied at different levels of granularity and\n   incorporates preferences for permissions, usage scope, and data\n   retention, providing a foundation for interoperability across various\n   Internet protocols.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2025-07-24T12:37:34Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/2440/", "id": 142662, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-vaughan-aipref-vocab", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 13, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-vaughan-aipref-vocab/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/", "/api/v1/doc/state/134/"], "std_level": null, "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/148596/"], "tags": [], "time": "2025-07-24T07:09:02Z", "title": "Vocabulary for Expressing Content Preferences for AI Training", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This Internet Draft proposes three categories that would enable\n   parties to express preferences regarding how digital assets are used\n   by automated processing systems.  The proposal is for these\n   categories to nest within the larger category of Automated\n   Processing, currently envisaged in the [AIPREF-VOCAB].\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-04-04T08:54:26Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 149814, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-romm-aipref-contentsignals", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 5, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-romm-aipref-contentsignals/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/155676/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-04-04T07:09:00Z", "title": "Vocabulary For Expressing Content Signals", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document proposes a standardized vocabulary of use cases that\n   can be targeted when expressing machine-readable opt-outs related to\n   Text and Data Mining (TDM) and AI training.  The vocabulary is\n   agnostic to specific opt-out mechanisms and enables declaring parties\n   to communicate restrictions or permissions regarding the use of their\n   digital assets in a structured and interoperable manner.  It defines\n   three key use cases—TDM, AI Training, and Generative AI\n   Training—which can be referenced by opt-out systems to ensure\n   consistent interpretation across different implementations.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2025-10-01T09:12:25Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/2440/", "id": 145272, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-keller-aipref-vocab", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 6, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-keller-aipref-vocab/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/4/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/", "/api/v1/doc/state/35/"], "std_level": null, "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/150867/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/150828/"], "tags": [], "time": "2025-04-10T14:46:31Z", "title": "Proposal for an Opt-Out Vocabulary", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies extensions to [CORE-SPEC], providing a wider\n   range of controls for server-side automation permissions.  It\n   supports rate limiting, automation technology restrictions, API\n   permissions, session requirements, and HTML asset annotations.  These\n   extensions enable service owners to exercise more granular control\n   over automated interactions.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2025-10-24T05:25:29Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 145485, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-liao-aipref-autoctl-ext", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 16, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-liao-aipref-autoctl-ext/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/151463/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/151152/"], "tags": [], "time": "2025-11-03T07:09:20Z", "title": "Protocol Extension for Automation Control", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document specifies a machine-readable protocol for server-side\n   automation permissions in the light of recent advances in AI-driven\n   web automation.  Building upon RFC9309, this protocol addresses a\n   broader range of state-changing activities that service owners may\n   wish to control.  It defines the file format, HTTP method\n   restrictions, and purpose requirements.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2025-10-23T07:36:36Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 145484, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-liao-aipref-autoctl-core", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 11, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-liao-aipref-autoctl-core/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/151435/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/151150/"], "tags": [], "time": "2025-11-03T07:09:20Z", "title": "Protocol for Automation Control", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Content creators and other stakeholders might wish to signal their\n   preferences about how their content might be consumed by automated\n   systems.  This document defines how preferences can be signaled as\n   part of the acquisition of content in HTTP.\n\n   This document updates RFC 9309 to allow for the inclusion of usage\n   preferences.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2025-11-29T01:55:19Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/2440/", "id": 146261, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-it-aipref-attachment", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 7, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-it-aipref-attachment/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/4/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/", "/api/v1/doc/state/35/"], "std_level": null, "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/152344/"], "tags": [], "time": "2025-05-28T02:45:54Z", "title": "Indicating Preferences Regarding Content Usage", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document describes best practices for web crawlers.\n\nDiscussion Venues\n\n   This note is to be removed before publishing as an RFC.\n\n   Source for this draft and an issue tracker can be found at\n   https://github.com/garyillyes/cbcp.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-10-11T19:36:49Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 146864, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-illyes-aipref-cbcp", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 7, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-illyes-aipref-cbcp/", "rev": "04", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/153559/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/155660/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/155784/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/161995/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/162036/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-11T07:09:03Z", "title": "Crawler best practices", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Content creators and other stakeholders might wish to signal their\n   preferences about how their content might be consumed by automated\n   systems.  This document defines a very simple format for expressions.\n\n   This document updates RFC 9309 to define one means of conveyance.  An\n   HTTP header field is also defined.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2025-08-21T04:48:33Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/2440/", "id": 143072, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-thomson-aipref-sup", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 17, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-thomson-aipref-sup/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/", "/api/v1/doc/state/134/"], "std_level": null, "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/149230/"], "tags": [], "time": "2025-08-20T07:09:01Z", "title": "Short Usage Preference Strings for Automated Processing", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines a vocabulary for expressing preferences\n   regarding how digital assets are used by automated processing\n   systems.  This vocabulary allows for the declaration of restrictions\n   or permissions for use of digital assets by such systems.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-03-18T06:18:02Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/2440/", "id": 145883, "intended_std_level": "/api/v1/name/intendedstdlevelname/ps/", "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-ietf-aipref-vocab", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 15, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-ietf-aipref-vocab/", "rev": "08", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/", "/api/v1/doc/state/38/"], "std_level": null, "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/151698/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/152854/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/154124/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/155077/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/157033/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/157845/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/162670/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/167868/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/169000/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-09-14T06:18:02Z", "title": "A Vocabulary For Expressing AI Usage Preferences", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Methods are defined for associating usage preferences with content\n   that is obtained using the HTTP protocol.  This document defines\n   attachment methods using the Robots Exclusion Protocol and HTTP\n   header fields.\n\n   This document updates RFC 9309 to allow for the inclusion of usage\n   preferences.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-02-20T04:25:16Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/2440/", "id": 146479, "intended_std_level": "/api/v1/name/intendedstdlevelname/ps/", "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-ietf-aipref-attach", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 10, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-ietf-aipref-attach/", "rev": "05", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/", "/api/v1/doc/state/38/"], "std_level": null, "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/152771/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/152853/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/154123/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/155076/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/157032/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/167869/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-19T04:25:16Z", "title": "Associating AI Usage Preferences with Content in HTTP", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This Internet Draft proposes a category entitled \"AI Substitutive\n   Use\" which would enable parties to express a preference regarding how\n   digital assets are used by automated processing systems, with a focus\n   on post-training (inference-time) uses that are likely to result in\n   the creation of AI-generated outputs that substitute for the original\n   asset.  The proposal is for this category to nest within the larger\n   category of Automated Processing, currently envisaged in the working\n   group draft [AIPREF-VOCAB] (21 July 2025).\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-03-12T21:05:18Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/2440/", "id": 149492, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-silver-aipref-vocab-substitutive", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 5, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-silver-aipref-vocab-substitutive/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/", "/api/v1/doc/state/134/"], "std_level": null, "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/155139/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-03-16T07:09:26Z", "title": "Vocabulary For Expressing AI Substitutive Usage", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document proposes a standardized vocabulary to express\n   preferences for usage of digital content collected by Search and AI\n   crawlers.  This vocabulary allows for the creation of structured\n   declarations about restrictions or permissions for use of content\n   retrieved by such systems.  Adding the No updates on March 24, 2026\n   to keep document from expiring.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-09-26T23:25:27Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/2440/", "id": 149645, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-madhavan-aipref-displaybasedpref", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 10, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-madhavan-aipref-displaybasedpref/", "rev": "02", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/", "/api/v1/doc/state/134/"], "std_level": null, "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/155407/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/155417/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/161617/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-09-26T07:09:02Z", "title": "A Vocabulary for Controlling Usage of Content Collected by Search and AI Crawlers", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines a standardized JSON format for operators of\n   automated HTTP clients (e.g., web crawlers, AI bots) to publicly\n   disclose their IP address ranges.  A consistent, machine-readable\n   format for IP range publication simplifies the task of identifying\n   and verifying legitimate automated traffic, thereby decreasing\n   maintenance load on website operators while reducing the risk of\n   inadvertently blocking beneficial clients.  This specification\n   codifies and extends common existing practices to provide a simple\n   yet extensible format that accommodates a variety of use cases.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-10-11T21:09:39Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 149792, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-illyes-aipref-jafar", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 8, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-illyes-aipref-jafar/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/4/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/155659/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/162038/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-04-09T21:09:39Z", "title": "A JSON-Based Format for Publishing IP Ranges of Automated HTTP Clients", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Standards can have a major impact on end users across technological,\n   legal, ethical, and governance dimensions, largely centering around\n   access to information, control over their digital contributions, and\n   data privacy.  The purpose of this Internet Draft is to document the\n   potential impact of signaling AI preferences on end users other than\n   publishers, and to suggest some principles for the ai-pref working\n   group to consider when assessing proposed vocabulary and definitions\n   IETF wishes to standardize for signaling.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-05-31T04:36:55Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 152542, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-farzdusa-aipref-enduser", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 13, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-farzdusa-aipref-enduser/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/157785/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-05-30T07:09:04Z", "title": "AI Preferences Signaling: End User Impact", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines how Artificial Intelligence (AI) preference\n   expressions are bound to signaling and media protocols used for real-\n   time, session-based communications such as the Session Initiation\n   Protocol (SIP) and associated Session Description Protocol (SDP)\n   offers.  It specifies a reusable binding model, concrete SIP header\n   field conventions, and SDP attributes that allow endpoints,\n   intermediary services, and AI assistants to advertise, negotiate, and\n   enforce requirements about AI-driven processing of session metadata,\n   media control events, and telemetry.  The goal is to align real-time\n   protocol behavior with the AI Preferences (AIPREF) vocabulary without\n   disrupting existing call control semantics.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-02-05T18:38:48Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 153484, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-altanai-aipref-realtime-protocol-bindings", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 21, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-altanai-aipref-realtime-protocol-bindings/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/167280/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/159304/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-04T18:38:48Z", "title": "AI Preferences for Real-Time Protocol Bindings", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document proposes an update to the AI preferences vocabulary\n   [VOCAB] in order to establish protected uses (exclusions).\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2026-10-24T16:58:57Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 155918, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-zehta-aipref-exclusions", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 5, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-zehta-aipref-exclusions/", "rev": "01", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/2/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/161948/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/162484/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-24T07:09:04Z", "title": "AIPREF Vocabulary Exclusions", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Work in the AI Preferences (AIPREF) Working Group defines a\n   vocabulary for expressing preferences about how digital assets may be\n   used by automated processing systems, together with mechanisms for\n   attaching those preferences to content.  Neither component provides a\n   way for a processing entity to demonstrate that it observed an\n   expressed preference, nor for a publisher or auditor to verify such a\n   demonstration after the fact.\n\n   This document defines the AI Usage Compliance Record (AUCR), a\n   structure that binds a retrieved asset, the preference expression in\n   force at the moment of retrieval, and the usage category the\n   processing entity assigned to that asset.  It defines an aggregation\n   scheme that allows a processing entity to attest to very large\n   numbers of records with a single signature, a proof mechanism that\n   allows an individual publisher to audit only the records concerning\n   its own assets, and a discovery mechanism for locating attestations\n   and verification keys.  The mechanism is deliberately confined to\n   evidence: it makes claims of compliance falsifiable and non-\n   repudiable, and takes no position on the legal effect of any\n   preference or any record.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-02-03T05:12:34Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 160114, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-reilly-aipref-compliance", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 19, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-reilly-aipref-compliance/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/167202/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-02T05:12:34Z", "title": "Verifiable Compliance Records for AI Usage Preferences", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document defines Earmark (Embedded Attribution and Rights\n   Marks), a mechanism by which publishers and rights holders embed\n   signed usage preferences directly into published content.  To earmark\n   content is to reserve it for designated uses, and the mark travels\n   with what it covers, surviving republication and aggregation, so the\n   preference remains discoverable wherever the content arrives,\n   including where perimeter signals such as robots.txt no longer apply.\n   Marks carry the identity of the rights holder, the preferences\n   asserted, and a signature, and are verifiable offline by any party.\n   An individual signed statement is a Mark; the mechanism as a whole is\n   Earmark.  This document defines the Mark Object, embedding bindings\n   for common content types, and the detection and verification\n   procedure.  It reuses the AI Preference vocabulary for preference\n   semantics and the C2PA and CAWG assertion infrastructure for media,\n   defining new machinery only where none exists.  Earmarks make ignored\n   preferences observable and attributable.  Enforcement remains with\n   law, contract, and the market.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-02-14T03:56:14Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 160387, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-hood-aipref-earmark", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 13, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-hood-aipref-earmark/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/167675/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-08-13T03:56:14Z", "title": "Earmark: Embedded Attribution and Rights Marks for AI Usage Preferences", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   The AI Preferences (AIPREF) vocabulary lets those with rights in a\n   digital asset express preferences -- for example, that training of AI\n   models is disallowed -- about how automated systems process that\n   asset.  Such a preference expresses a reservation.  It does not, by\n   itself, provide a verifiable, revocable record of a specific grant\n   that lifts a preference for a specific party.\n\n   This document describes that gap and proposes a candidate mechanism:\n   a cryptographically signed, offline-verifiable credential that\n   expresses a grant referencing an AIPREF usage category and a specific\n   asset, that any party can verify without contacting the grantor, and\n   that the grantor can revoke.  It is intended as a starting point for\n   discussion, not as a finished specification.\n\n   The mechanism is preference-general.  Training is used throughout as\n   the worked example because it is the reservation most widely\n   discussed, but nothing in the construction is specific to it: the\n   credential binds whichever usage category was reserved to a named\n   party, and the same procedure applies to any other category the\n   vocabulary expresses.  What the mechanism establishes is that a grant\n   exists, is authentic, and was in force and unrevoked at a stated\n   evaluation time -- or that the evidence available does not determine\n   this, which it reports as a distinct outcome rather than guessing.\n   It does not adjudicate whether the grantor had standing to grant, and\n   it is not an enforcement or access-control mechanism.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-04-30T11:26:27Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 157681, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-wallace-aipref-grant-binding", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 15, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-wallace-aipref-grant-binding/", "rev": "03", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/167390/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/165458/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/167842/", "/api/v1/submit/submission/171048/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-27T11:26:27Z", "title": "A Verifiable-Credential Binding for AI Usage Preferences: Expressing Grants that Lift AIPREF Preferences", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   This document describes how a service operator binds usage\n   preferences (e.g., \"no AI training\") to paths by writing ordinary\n   robots.txt groups keyed by a per-purpose policy token in the User-\n   Agent line.  It introduces no new directive names and no new grammar:\n   the path language, longest-match precedence, and group structure of\n   robots.txt are not changed.\n", "ad": null, "expires": "2027-05-03T15:12:37Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1027/", "id": 162065, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-kelley-aipref-training-preference-signal", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 10, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-kelley-aipref-training-preference-signal/", "rev": "00", "rfc": null, "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/"], "std_level": null, "stream": null, "submissions": ["/api/v1/submit/submission/171372/"], "tags": [], "time": "2026-10-30T15:12:37Z", "title": "Machine-Readable Training Preference Signal", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}]}